## PART 2: ENGLISH EDITION

### I. Contextual Framing & Video Forensic Analysis
In file **"29761.mp4"**, a spokesperson representing the Myanmar Military Commission addresses the international press regarding the continued diplomatic isolation of detained former State Counsellor Daw Aung San Suu Kyi. The exchange highlights a rigid, transactional deployment of domestic law designed to completely block international mediation.

#### Dual-Language Verification Transcript (Source: 29761.mp4)
> "This is regarding Daw Aung San Suu Kyi, and whether or not the ASEAN Special Envoy will be granted permission to meet her. Regarding this question, Daw Aung San Suu Kyi is an individual who has been legally penalized under criminal charges and is currently serving her sentence. For this reason, she is not permitted to meet with international envoys. Only after her prison sentence has been fully served might such a meeting be possible. At this present time, because she is actively serving her sentence, permission to meet cannot be granted."

### II. Strategic Analysis: The Mechanics of Unfair Detention
For ordinary citizens and local leaders across the towns of our liberated areas, understanding this statement requires looking directly past the polite bureaucratic tone. The script reveals three core mechanisms of narrative warfare used by the Military Commission (စစ်ကော်မရှင်):

1. **The Manufactured Criminal Mask (The Legalistic Shield)**  
   By repeating the phrase *"legally penalized under criminal charges,"* the spokesperson attempts to strip away the political reality of the coup. The administration treats politically motivated charges as standard judicial administrative facts to shield an unfair, arbitrary arrest behind a veneer of routine civil law.
2. **Institutionalized Delay as a Diplomatic Weapon**  
   The assertion that a meeting might occur *"only after her prison sentence has been fully served"* is a direct manifestation of institutionalized delay. By dangling a theoretical, far-future possibility, the commission aims to exhaust international patience and dilute the urgency of ASEAN's Five-Point Consensus.
3. **Deliberate Isolation from Global Oversight**  
   Denying the ASEAN Special Envoy access under the guise of domestic rules signals a complete rejection of external verification. The administration uses domestic legal technicalities to insulate its political decisions from international eyes, keeping the details of this unfair detention locked away from external accountability.

